            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Francis Drake</strong><br>\nFrancis Drake was born around 1540 in Devonshire, England. His family was Protestant, which influenced his views during a time of religious conflict. Drake started sailing at an early age, which sparked his lifelong passion for exploration and seafaring. Early exposure to the sea, as well as his cousin Sir John Hawkins, played a significant role in his maritime career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Drake's Seafaring Career Begins</strong><br>\nDrake began his seafaring career by working on trading ships in the English Channel. In the 1560s, he made his first voyages to the Americas with his cousin, Hawkins. This experience was crucial in developing his navigation skills and understanding of the lucrative opportunities in the New World, which later motivated his voyages and raids against the Spanish.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Voyage to the Caribbean</strong><br>\nIn the 1570s, Drake embarked on his first independent voyage to the Caribbean. He attacked Spanish ports with a small fleet, capturing significant amounts of treasure. This expedition established his reputation as a formidable privateer and earned him the nickname \"El Draque\" or \"The Dragon\" by the Spaniards.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Circumnavigation of the Globe</strong><br>\nDrake's most famous voyage began in 1577 when he set sail to circumnavigate the globe. He was the first Englishman to complete the journey, returning to England in 1580 with a ship full of spices and captured Spanish treasures. Queen Elizabeth I knighted Drake aboard his ship, the Golden Hind, for his contributions to England's wealth and status.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conflict with Spain</strong><br>\nDrake's success as a privateer heightened tensions between England and Spain. His raids on Spanish treasure fleets and towns were effectively state-sponsored piracy. These acts were among the causes leading up to the Anglo-Spanish War and the Spanish Armada's attempted invasion of England in 1588.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Spanish Armada and Battle Tactics</strong><br>\nDuring the Spanish Armada crisis in 1588, Drake played a crucial role in the English victory. His leadership and innovative naval tactics, such as using fire ships to break the Spanish formation, were instrumental in defeating the seemingly invincible Spanish fleet, protecting England from invasion.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
