            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Expeditions</strong><br>\nAfter the defeat of the Spanish Armada, Drake continued to lead English fleets in aggressive actions against Spanish interests. His later expeditions included unsuccessful attempts to capture Spanish territories in Portugal and the Americas. These failures, however, did little to tarnish his heroic status in England.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Final Voyage and Death</strong><br>\nDrake's final voyage to the West Indies in 1595 aimed to replicate his earlier successes but ended in failure. After a series of defeats and suffering from dysentery, Drake died on January 28, 1596, aboard his ship off the coast of Panama. His body was dressed in full armor and buried at sea in a lead coffin.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Sir Francis Drake</strong><br>\nSir Francis Drake's legacy is complex. He is celebrated as a hero in England for his explorations, contributions to the defeat of the Spanish Armada, and the riches he brought home. However, in Spain, he is remembered as a feared pirate. His actions at sea changed naval warfare and set a precedent for future English sea dominance.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
